---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import CtaRow from "../../components/CtaRow.astro";
import { linkClass } from "../../lib/links";
import { withBase } from "../../lib/base";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Firearm and Accessory Sales Trends in Q1 2025 | FFL Accelerator"
  description="Q1 2025 firearms conversation: cooler unit demand after the 2020–23 surge, concealed-carry and training SKUs still working, AR and bulk ammo softer."
  path="/trends/2025-q1/"
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 py-12 sm:py-14">
    <p
      class="mb-3 text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-gold"
    >
      Trends · Q1 2025
    </p>
    <h1 class="mb-4 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-white">
      Firearm and accessory sales trends in Q1 2025
    </h1>
    <p class="mb-8 text-sm leading-relaxed text-zinc-300">
      The first quarter of 2025 felt like a continuation of the post-surge
      hangover: fewer panic tickets, more shopping around, and a floor that
      still moved concealed-carry pistols and cheap practice ammo. That is a
      merchandising read — not a NICS print and not a RetailBI Index number.
      If you need a sourced 2025 unit series, use the published ones and keep
      them labeled.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">What the quarter felt like</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-zinc-400">
      Dealers we talk to described a selective buyer: self-defense pistols and
      the holster / optic / mag ecosystem around them, plus .22 LR for range
      time, while long-gun walls and thousand-round cases sat longer. Mid-tier
      handguns still did the work; premium “collect the rifle” tickets were
      quieter. SHOT-week gadgets showed up in conversation more than they
      showed up as volume.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-lg font-semibold text-white">Categories that still worked</h2>
    <ul class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-sm text-zinc-400">
      <li>
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Concealed-carry pistols.</strong> Micro-
        and compact 9mms — P365-class, 43X-class, Shield-class — remained the
        default first answer for walk-ins. Aftermarket grip modules, optic
        plates, and AIWB holsters rode along.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">.22 LR trainers.</strong> When centerfire
        practice felt expensive, the 10/22 and a brick of rimfire were the
        honest bundle.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Small accessories, not another rifle.</strong>
        Slides, threaded barrels, lasers, and ear pro are where a slow unit
        quarter can still print margin — if the site and the counter can find
        them.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-lg font-semibold text-white">Categories that sat</h2>
    <ul class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-sm text-zinc-400">
      <li>
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">AR-pattern rifles.</strong> After years of
        surge buying, first-rifle demand was not the story. Discounting a stale
        wall is merchandising, not a national series.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Pump shotguns.</strong> Home-defense
        shoppers kept asking about softer-shooting semis. Pumps did not vanish;
        they stopped being automatic.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Bulk ammo cases.</strong> Fewer “fill the
        closet” tickets. More 50- and 100-round buys for this weekend.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-lg font-semibold text-white">
      If you mention 2025 units, say which number
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-zinc-400">
      NSSF, citing Gearfire’s 2025 Annual Industry Report (23 Feb 2026), put
      same-store new firearm units at about <strong class="text-zinc-200"
        >−13% vs 2024</strong
      >. That is a register / same-store print for the <em class="not-italic"
        >year</em
      >, not a Q1 NICS total and not “what people typed.” We are not reprinting
      a monthly RetailBI Index here.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-10 text-sm leading-relaxed text-zinc-300">
      If the floor is moving and the website is not, that is the FFL
      Accelerator job. $569 a month. You own the site.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-6">
      <CtaRow />
    </div>

    <p class="mt-10 text-sm text-zinc-500">
      Next:{" "}
      <a href={withBase("trends/2025-q2")} class={linkClass}>Q2 2025 →</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
